// billion digits, which is what lets the comparisons below be total: every
// number the JSON grammar allows has an answer here, at a cost set by the
// length of the literal rather than by its magnitude. A parse into float64
// cannot be asked these questions at all -- it holds 2^53 and 2^53+1 as one
// number, and holds 1e400 as an infinity -- which is the whole reason the value
// is kept as its literal.
//
// A zero of any spelling -- 0, -0.0, 0e100 -- answers with no digits at all,
// and its sign goes with them. JSON Schema has no -0 distinct from 0:
// {"minimum":0} admits -0.0, and float64, which does keep the sign, agrees only
// because -0.0 < 0 is false there too.
